he correct answer.\n\nJust making sure there are no miscalculations. Let me do the multiplication step by step again:\n\nFirst step: 61 \u00d7 0 = 0.\n\nSecond step: 61 \u00d7 40. Breaking it down:\n\n61 \u00d7 40 = 61 \u00d7 (4 \u00d7 10) = (61 \u00d7 4) \u00d7 10.\n\n61 \u00d7 4:\n\n60 \u00d7 4 = 240,\n\n1 \u00d7 4 = 4,\n\n240 + 4 = 244.\n\nThen multiply by 10: 244 \u00d7 10 = 2440.\n\nAdding the two steps: 0 + 2440 = 2440.\n\nEverything checks out. So the answer is 2440, and the reasoning follows the structure provided in the example.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe final computed product is \\boxed{2440}.\n\n\nTo find the product of 61 and 40, we break down the second number (40) into its place values and multiply each by the first number (61), then sum the results.\n\n1. Multiply 61 by the ones place digit (0) of 40:\n   \\[\n   61 \\times 0 = 0\n   \\]\n\n2. Multiply 61 by the tens place digit (40) of 40:\n   \\[\n   61 \\times 40 = 61 \\times (4 \\times 10) = (61 \\times 4) \\times 10 = 244 \\times 10 = 2440\n   \\]\n\nSumming the results of the two steps:\n\\[\n0 + 2440 = 2440\n\\]\n\nThus, the final computed product is \\boxed{2440}.",
