 thinking of a different problem, but now it's clear.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 66 and 40 is \\boxed{2640}.\n\n\nTo find the product of 66 and 40, we can break down the multiplication step by step based on the digits of the second number (40).\n\n1. **Multiply 66 by the ones place digit (0):**\n   \\[\n   66 \\times 0 = 0\n   \\]\n\n2. **Multiply 66 by the tens place digit (4) considering its place value (40):**\n   \\[\n   66 \\times 40 = 66 \\times (4 \\times 10) = (66 \\times 4) \\times 10 = 264 \\times 10 = 2640\n   \\]\n\n**Sum all the step results:**\n\\[\n0 + 2640 = 2640\n\\]\n\nThus, the final computed product is \\(\\boxed{2640}\\).",
