\u00d79: 80\u00d79=720, 8\u00d79=72. 720+72=792. Then \u00d710 is 7920. So adding 704 +7920. \n\nWait, 704 +7920: 700+7920= 8620, plus 4 is 8624. But the other method gave me 7744. Which is correct?\n\nHmm, I must have messed up the second method's setup. Wait, the original problem is 88 \u00d798. So 98 can be written as 100 -2. So 88\u00d7(100-2)=88\u00d7100 -88\u00d72=8800 -176= 8624. Ah, there we go! I see, earlier when I did (90-2)\u00d788, that was incorrect. 98 is 90+8, not 100-2. Wait no, 98 is 100-2. Wait, 90+8 is 98, but 100-2 is also 98. So both are correct. Wait but in my first alternative method I used 90-2? Wait no, I think I made a mistake there.\n\nWait, let me clarify. \n\nFirst method: 88 \u00d7 98. Breaking 98 into 90 +8, so 88\u00d790 +88\u00d78 = 7920 +704= 8624.\n\nSecond method: 98 as 100-2: 88\u00d7100=8800, minus 88\u00d72=176, so 8800-176=8624. Correct. \n\nWait earlier when I did (90-2) \u00d788, that would be 86\u00d788, not 98\u00d788. So that was my mistake. I must have confused the numbers. So the correct answer is 8624.\n\nTherefore, the standard multiplication steps give the correct result. So the problem in my first alternative approach was due to an incorrect decomposition. So the correct answer is 8624.\n\nTherefore, going back to the required template.\n\nThe user wants the product of 88 and 98. Following the steps:\n\nStep 1: Multiply 88 by the ones digit of 98, which is 8. 88\u00d78=704.\n\nStep 2: Multiply 88 by the tens digit of 9 (which is actually 90). So 88\u00d79=792, then add a zero to make it 7920.\n\nAdding those two results: 704 +7920 = 8624. So that's the answer.\n\nWait but in the example given in the problem statement, the template had three steps because the second number was 153 (three digits). Since 98 is two digits, the reasoning should have two steps. Let me adjust accordingly.\n\nOriginal example:\n\nFor 27 \u00d7153:\n\n1. Multiply 27\u00d73 (ones place)\n2. Multiply 27\u00d750 (tens place)\n3. Multiply 27\u0