lication way:\n\n     45\n  x 41\n  ------\n     45 (45 * 1)\n  1800 (45 * 40, shifted one position to the left)\n  ------\n  1845\n\nYes, that\'s correct. So the answer is 1845. I think that\'s right. Let me verify with another method.\n\nAlternatively, 45*40 is 1800, plus 45 more is 1845. Yep. So the steps are correct.\n\nTherefore, the final answer should be 1845.\n\nBut let me make sure I didn\'t make any arithmetic errors. Let\'s compute 45*40 again:\n\n45 multiplied by 4 is 180, so times 10 is 1800. Correct.\n\nAdding 45 gives 1800 +45 = 1845. Yes, that\'s correct.\n\nAlright, so the reasoning steps are properly laid out.\n</think>\n\n# Question:\nWhat is the product of 45 and 41?\n# Reasoning:\nLet\'s think step by step. Since 41 has two digits, we can break the multiplication into two steps:\n1. **Multiply 45 by the ones place digit (1):**  \n   \\(45 \\times 1 = 45\\).  \n2. **Multiply 45 by the tens place digit (4), treating it as 40:**  \n   \\(45 \\times 40 = 1,800\\).  \n\n**Sum the results of the steps:**  \n\\(45 + 1,\\!800 = 1,\\!845\\).  \n\nAnswer:  \nNow, sum all the step results: \\(45 + 1,\\!800 = 1,\\!845\\).  \nSo, the final computed product is \\boxed{1845}.', '']
